=F0=9F=91=8B  Good morning and happy 2026 to you. We=E2=80=99re only two da=
ys in, but we can already tell it=E2=80=99s shaping up to be a good one. Be=
fore you blend up your green juice, write in your mindfulness journal, and =
hit the gym, take a second to check out our predictions for the glorious ye=
ar in business ahead.

OUR CRYSTAL BALL

Top storylines for 2026

This past year was perhaps one of the wildest we can remember, and that=E2=
=80=99s largely because it=E2=80=99s the first where we=E2=80=99ve had to a=
sk ourselves if every cute animal video we saw was real or just an AI fever=
 dream. (That possum trying to get the Halloween candy? Yeah, fake. We were=
 pissed.)

Now we=E2=80=99re looking ahead and, to be perfectly honest, we don=E2=80=
=99t see things getting any less weird:

Robots are performing more labor, perhaps including actual labor

The past few years have given us a robot for everything, from folding cloth=
es to serving drinks to tamping down grain in a silo, and we=E2=80=99re sur=
e to see even more when CES returns to Las Vegas Jan. 6-9. But will they ev=
er give birth?

Kaiwa Technology (https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/international/u=
s/chinas-kaiwa-technology-develops-pregnancy-humanoid-robot-with-artificial=
-womb-technology/articleshow/123358906.cms?from=3Dmdr&_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8ZCB=
Lrj0V-8PY-A50P0BOgHP8yJdaFKTZkY_OmNClDFz4xVKzzu96utVpM5fM5_h-nuKmxrPzkGNct3=
sLsvgdkikgKpg&_hsmi=3D395310945 ) , a Chinese tech company, claimed in Augu=
st that it expected to launch a robot with a synthetic womb designed to car=
ry a baby from embryo until birth in 2026. It would cost ~$14k (https://www=
.vice.com/en/article/robot-with-artificial-womb-could-give-birth-to-humans-=
by-next-year/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8ZCBLrj0V-8PY-A50P0BOgHP8yJdaFKTZkY_OmNClDFz=
4xVKzzu96utVpM5fM5_h-nuKmxrPzkGNct3sLsvgdkikgKpg&_hsmi=3D395310945 ) , so n=
ot exactly cheap, but cheaper than a human surrogate, who is typically paid=
 between $100k and $200k per pregnancy. Responses to the bot are predictabl=
y mixed.

While we do expect to see robots and AI models infiltrate more of our lives=
 than perhaps we want them to, this robot may be entirely sci-fi for now. I=
t=E2=80=99s entirely unclear how exactly the bot becomes pregnant or deals =
with the numerous complicated elements of a pregnancy, so we wouldn=E2=80=
=99t hold our breath on this one.

$1T shopping spree

We=E2=80=99ll find out if the National Retail Federation=E2=80=99s predicti=
on (https://www.axios.com/2025/11/06/holiday-sales-2025-nrf-forecast-trilli=
on-dollar?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8ZCBLrj0V-8PY-A50P0BOgHP8yJdaFKTZkY_OmNClDFz4xVK=
zzu96utVpM5fM5_h-nuKmxrPzkGNct3sLsvgdkikgKpg&_hsmi=3D395310945 ) =E2=80=94 =
that US holiday shopping will exceed $1T for the first time ever in 2025 =
=E2=80=94 came true. NRF estimated that shoppers dropped an average of ~$89=
0 per person on gifts, food, and decor, the second-highest since NRF began =
tracking 23 years ago.

Granted, the price of nearly everything has gone up, but it was still an od=
d year for people to spend big. Yet retailers rolled out sales earlier and =
did what they could to keep prices low, and while shoppers hunted for barga=
ins and switched to cheaper brands, they still prioritized spending on gift=
s. After all, people need some joy in their lives.

Economists are feeling mid, as the kids say, about US economic growth going=
 into 2026, but do expect inflation to ease gradually while still remaining=
 over the Fed=E2=80=99s 2% goal, per (https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articl=
es/2025-08-29/economists-see-slow-us-growth-stubborn-inflation-well-into-20=
26?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8ZCBLrj0V-8PY-A50P0BOgHP8yJdaFKTZkY_OmNClDFz4xVKzzu96ut=
VpM5fM5_h-nuKmxrPzkGNct3sLsvgdkikgKpg&_hsmi=3D395310945 ) Bloomberg.

Agentic everything

Everyone=E2=80=99s had plenty of time to ask AI to generate images of cats =
playing banjos and recipes that may or may not be edible, but companies are=
 now looking to agentic AI (https://thehustle.co/news/the-future-of-web-bro=
wsers?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8ZCBLrj0V-8PY-A50P0BOgHP8yJdaFKTZkY_OmNClDFz4xVKzzu9=
6utVpM5fM5_h-nuKmxrPzkGNct3sLsvgdkikgKpg&_hsmi=3D395310945 ) , which can ma=
ke decisions and perform autonomous tasks on behalf of its user.

Some examples you=E2=80=99re likely to see more of this year:

- Self-driving vehicles: Agentic AI is behind the wheel. Waymo rolled out i=
n numerous cities in 2025, and started operating (https://techcrunch.com/20=
25/11/20/waymo-enters-3-more-cities-minneapolis-new-orleans-and-tampa/?_hse=
nc=3Dp2ANqtz-8ZCBLrj0V-8PY-A50P0BOgHP8yJdaFKTZkY_OmNClDFz4xVKzzu96utVpM5fM5=
_h-nuKmxrPzkGNct3sLsvgdkikgKpg&_hsmi=3D395310945 )  in Minneapolis, New Orl=
eans, and Tampa, Florida, with human safety drivers. It will also allow ful=
ly autonomous rides in Miami, Dallas, Houston, San Antonio, and Orlando in =
2026. There may be some resistance, however. San Franciscans were outraged =
(https://www.nytimes.com/2025/11/15/us/waymo-san-francisco-kit-kat.html?_hs=
enc=3Dp2ANqtz-8ZCBLrj0V-8PY-A50P0BOgHP8yJdaFKTZkY_OmNClDFz4xVKzzu96utVpM5fM=
5_h-nuKmxrPzkGNct3sLsvgdkikgKpg&_hsmi=3D395310945 ) when a Waymo ran over a=
 beloved neighborhood cat. One city supervisor has asked for locals to have=
 more control over how robotaxis are deployed in their communities.

- Browsers: Agentic web browsers including Perplexity=E2=80=99s Comet, Chat=
GPT=E2=80=99s Atlas, and Opera=E2=80=99s Neon seek to make web browsing a s=
eamless experience where AI can do things like fill out forms, summarize co=
ntent, and complete bookings for their users. Other browsers, including Goo=
gle Chrome and Mozilla=E2=80=99s Firefox, are incorporating AI.

Such browsers may threaten Chrome=E2=80=99s long-standing dominance if they=
 achieve widespread adoption, but reviews are currently mixed. PC Mag=E2=80=
=99s Ruben Circelli found (https://www.pcmag.com/opinions/ive-tested-too-ma=
ny-ai-web-browsers-that-all-have-the-same-fatal-flaws?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8ZCB=
Lrj0V-8PY-A50P0BOgHP8yJdaFKTZkY_OmNClDFz4xVKzzu96utVpM5fM5_h-nuKmxrPzkGNct3=
sLsvgdkikgKpg&_hsmi=3D395310945 ) they required too much babysitting to out=
perform traditional browsers. And for people with data privacy concerns, Du=
ckDuckGo remains a favorite.

- Shopping: Amazon rolled out its =E2=80=9CBuy for Me (https://www.coveo.co=
m/blog/the-rise-of-agentic-commerce/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8ZCBLrj0V-8PY-A50P0BO=
gHP8yJdaFKTZkY_OmNClDFz4xVKzzu96utVpM5fM5_h-nuKmxrPzkGNct3sLsvgdkikgKpg&_hs=
mi=3D395310945 ) =E2=80=9D feature to select users in 2025, which lets cust=
omers quickly purchase items not just from Amazon, but third-party websites=
, without leaving the platform. OpenAI and other companies are already inte=
grating with platforms like Etsy to allow shoppers to browse and buy produc=
ts entirely within platforms like ChatGPT. Combine this tech with AR try-on=
s that let you visualize what outfits might look like on you or how a piece=
 of furniture might look in your home, and you=E2=80=99ve got a pretty high=
-tech retail experience brewing.

- Travel: Agentic AI is also expected to make its way into the travel secto=
r, with 80% (https://www.nytimes.com/2025/11/25/travel/what-is-agentic-ai-b=
ook-flights.html?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8ZCBLrj0V-8PY-A50P0BOgHP8yJdaFKTZkY_OmNCl=
DFz4xVKzzu96utVpM5fM5_h-nuKmxrPzkGNct3sLsvgdkikgKpg&_hsmi=3D395310945 ) of =
travel execs planning to roll out agentic AI tools at scale within the next=
 five years. Such assistants would ideally find flights and lodging options=
 based on your preferences, compare prices, come up with itineraries, and h=
andle booking.

To the moon

No, we=E2=80=99re not talking about crypto, thankfully. We=E2=80=99re talki=
ng about literally going to the moon. Artemis II (https://www.nasa.gov/miss=
ion/artemis-ii/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8ZCBLrj0V-8PY-A50P0BOgHP8yJdaFKTZkY_OmNClD=
Fz4xVKzzu96utVpM5fM5_h-nuKmxrPzkGNct3sLsvgdkikgKpg&_hsmi=3D395310945 ) is t=
he second of NASA=E2=80=99s Artemis missions, intended to build a long-term=
 presence on the moon. Scheduled to launch in early 2026, Artemis II will b=
e the first crewed flight test of NASA=E2=80=99s Space Launch System (SLS) =
rocket and Orion spacecraft around the moon, marking the first time humans =
have traveled to the moon since 1972. Humans won=E2=80=99t actually set foo=
t on the moon again until Artemis III, scheduled for some time in 2027.

